spoon, and fork.\par 8.\emdash  What do you wish to eat? Here is the menu (}{\i\f211 list of-foods}{\f208 ).\par 9.\emdash  Little Victor takes the menu and answers:\par 10.\emdash  I want (}{\i\f211 wish-for}{\f208 ) lobster \u224\'88 la armoricaine (}{\i\f211 in Armorican sauce}{\f208 ), then roasted hare or rabbit chasseur (}{\i\f211 in hunting manner}{\f208 ), [and] finally apple pie (}{\i\f211 pastry with apples}{\f208 ).\par 11.\emdash  The angered mother slaps Victor (}{\i\f211 leads a slap to Victor}{\f208 ), saying:\par 12.\emdash  You want too many things. Bread, eggs, [and] cheese will be enough for us.\par }{\f208\cf13 \par }{\f208 NOTES\par }{\b\f210 1. Ass}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 dite}{\f208 : imperative, 2}{\f208\super nd}{\f208  pers. pl. of }{\b\f210 ass}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 do}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 is}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 ere}{\f208 ), }{\i\f211 to sit down}{\f208 . }{\b\f210 M}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 nsa}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 the table on which one eats}{\f208 ; do you recall the }{\b\f210 t}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 bula multiplicat}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ria}{\f208  (L 6, N 1).\par }{\b\f210 2.}{\f208  A}{\b\f210 ffer}{\f208 , imperative, 2}{\f208\super nd}{\f208  pers. sg. of a}{\b\f210 ffero}{\f208 , composed of }{\b\f210 ad}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 towards}{\f208 , and }{\b\f210 f}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 ro}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 fers}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 f}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 rre}{\f208 ), }{\i\f211 to carry}{\f208 ; and conjugated as the latter. }{\b\f210 F}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 ro}{\f208  and its compounds have their place in the series of }{\b\i\f212 irregular verbs}{\f208 . Do not forget that in all languages, by virtue of the law of maximum inconvenience, }{\i\f211 it is precisely the irregular verbs that are the most often used}{\f208  (if they were not used much, they would be replaced sooner or later by regular verbs). Moreover, you should not be afraid to use them, even if you get muddled in their conjugation. Little by little, they will become familiar to you, and as these verbs are very useful, your efforts will be rewarded.\par }{\b\f210 3.}{\f208  O}{\b\f210 mnia necess}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 ria}{\f208  is a neuter plural: }{\i\f211 all things necessary}{\f208 . You will see later that this form is easily recognizable from the form in }{\b\f210 a}{\f208  of the feminine singular, thanks to the other words of the sentence, and even here thanks to o}{\b\f210 mnia}{\f208  which can only be the neuter plural of the pronoun o}{\b\f210 mnis}{\f208  that we saw in the previous lesson.\par }{\b\f210 4. Fuscinul}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 mque}{\f208  = }{\b\f210 et fusc}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 nulam}{\f208 . Note that the adjective u}{\b\f210 nam}{\f208  agrees in gender and number with the last term of the enumeration. This is not the only possible construction: we will return to this later. Also notice that the accent migrates to the syllable which precedes the particle }{\b\f210 que}{\f208 !\par }{\b\f210 5. Loc}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 sta}{\f208  means at the same time }{\i\f211 lobster}{\f208  (notice the resemblance of both words) and }{\i\f211 locust}{\f208 .\par }{\b\f210 6.}{\f208\super  }{\f208  }{\b\f210 L}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 porem}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 hare}{\f208 , direct object; if it were a subject, it would be }{\b\f210 l}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 pus}{\f208 ; whereas }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rtus}{\f208 , subject, makes }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rtum}{\f208 , object: all words do not vary in the same way. Have patience! What seems disturbing now will soon seem natural to you.\par }{\b\f210 7.}{\f208  }{\b\f210 Cr}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 stulum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 cake}{\f208 , (diminutive of }{\b\f210 cr}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 sta}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 the crust}{\f208 ) is neuter.\par }{\b\f210 8. N}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 mia}{\f208 , literally }{\i\f211 too many things}{\f208 , is a neuter adjective: }{\b\f210 v}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 de s}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 pra n}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 tam t}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 rtiam}{\f208  (}{\i\f211 see note 3 above}{\f208 ). }{\b\f210 Vid. sup.}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 see above}{\f208 , is a frequent abbreviation.\par }{\b\f210 9.}{\f208  O}{\b\f210 va}{\f208 . Still a neuter plural (n. pl.). The singular is o}{\b\f210 vum}{\f208 . If you know Italian, think that the plural of }{\b\f210 uovo}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 the egg}{\f208 , is irregular: }{\b\f210 uova}{\f208 ; but it comes directly from a regular Latin form.\par For }{\b\f210 C}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 seum}{\f208 , think of the German }{\i\f211 K\u228\'8ase}{\f208  or the English }{\i\f211 cheese}{\f208 . Whereas the French word (}{\i\f211 fromage}{\f208 ) and its Italian counterpart (}{\i\f211 formaggio}{\f208 ) come from another Latin root: }{\b\f210 f}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rma}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 form}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 mould}{\f208 .\par You can definitely see that we do not leave the European family!\par \par EXERCITATIO\par \par 1.\emdash  Mother puts a pastry on the plate.\par 2.\emdash  The servant brings the hare into the dining room.\par 3.\emdash  The servants bring the hares into the dining rooms.\par 4.\emdash  The hare costs six francs.\par 5.\emdash  The hares don\rquote t wish to have lunch in the dining room.\par 6.\emdash  How much does the lobster cost? \emdash  Ten francs!\par 7.\emdash  It costs too much.\par 8.\emdash  What is this dish (}{\i\f211 food}{\f208 )?\par 9.\emdash  I can\rquote t find the dish I want (}{\i\f211 wish}{\f208 ) on the menu.}{\par }}